    <section id="overview"><h2>Week 5 overview</h2>
<p>Optimization is the part of training that changes model parameters so the loss becomes smaller. A model makes a prediction, a loss measures how wrong it is, backpropagation calculates gradients, and an optimizer uses those gradients to update parameters.</p><p>Keep these roles separate: <strong>loss function</strong> says how bad a prediction is; <strong>gradient</strong> says how the loss changes with a parameter; <strong>optimizer</strong> decides how to use the gradient to update that parameter.</p>
</section>
<section id="gradient"><h2>Gradient descent and the update rule</h2>
<p>For parameters \(\theta\), loss \(L(\theta)\), learning rate \(\eta\), and gradient \(\nabla_\theta L\), gradient descent updates in the opposite direction of the gradient:</p><div class="eq">\(\theta_{t+1}=\theta_t-\eta\nabla_\theta L(\theta_t)\)</div><p>If the gradient is positive, the parameter decreases. If the gradient is negative, subtracting it increases the parameter. The learning rate sets the step size; it is a hyperparameter, not a learned weight.</p><h3>Worked example</h3><p>For \(L(w)=w^2\), the derivative is \(2w\). At \(w=3\) with learning rate 0.1, the gradient is 6 and the new value is \(3-0.1(6)=2.4\).</p><p>In PyTorch, the usual order is <code>optimizer.zero_grad()</code>, forward pass, loss calculation, <code>loss.backward()</code>, then <code>optimizer.step()</code>. Clearing gradients matters because PyTorch accumulates them by default.</p>
</section>
<section id="convex"><h2>Convex optimization: local and global minima</h2>
<p>A function is convex when the line segment between any two points on its graph lies on or above the graph; intuitively, it has a bowl-like shape. For a differentiable convex objective, any local minimum is also a global minimum. For a non-convex neural-network loss, there may be many valleys, saddle points and flat regions, so a local optimization method is not generally guaranteed to find the global best parameters.</p>
<p>Gradient descent uses only local slope information. It is not the same as an exhaustive search. The gradient is zero at a stationary point, but a stationary point may be a minimum, maximum or saddle point. In high-dimensional deep learning, saddle regions and poorly conditioned directions can make optimization slow.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Exam distinction:</strong> convexity is a property of the objective function; gradient descent is an algorithm used to optimize it. A convex loss does not mean every possible learning rate is safe.</div></section>\n<section id="variants"><h2>Batch, stochastic and mini-batch gradient descent</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Samples per update</th><th>Typical behaviour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Batch GD</td><td>Entire training set</td><td>Stable gradient estimate; expensive update on large datasets.</td></tr><tr><td>Stochastic GD (SGD)</td><td>One example</td><td>Frequent, noisy updates; can be computationally light per update.</td></tr><tr><td>Mini-batch GD</td><td>A small batch, e.g. 32 or 128</td><td>Balances efficient hardware use and gradient noise; common default.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>One epoch means the model has processed every training example once. If there are \(N\) examples and batch size \(B\), updates per epoch are usually \(\lceil N/B\rceil\) when the final partial batch is retained, or \(\lfloor N/B\rfloor\) if it is dropped.</p>
</section>
<section id="learning-rate"><h2>Learning rate and schedules</h2>
<p>A learning rate that is too small makes progress slow. One that is too large can overshoot a minimum, oscillate, or diverge. A schedule changes the learning rate during training, for example by reducing it after a fixed number of epochs or when validation performance plateaus.</p><p>Do not assume that a larger learning rate always trains faster. The useful step size depends on the loss landscape, parameter scale, optimizer and training stage.</p>
</section>
<section id="schedules"><h2>Learning-rate schedules and accelerated methods</h2>
<p>A schedule changes the learning rate over time. Common patterns include step decay (reduce it at chosen epochs), exponential decay (multiply it by a factor repeatedly), cosine decay (gradually follow a cosine-shaped curve), and reducing the rate when validation performance plateaus. Warm-up starts with a smaller rate and increases it during early training, often helping large models train stably.</p>
<p>Momentum is an accelerated method in the sense that it accumulates a velocity from past gradients. Nesterov momentum evaluates the gradient after a look-ahead step, giving a different correction. Adam combines momentum-like first-moment tracking with adaptive scaling from the second moment. These methods can improve optimization but remain sensitive to learning rate and task.</p>
<p>Do not confuse an epoch-based schedule with batch size: the schedule changes the step size, while batch size changes how many examples contribute to each gradient estimate.</p></section>\n<section id="optimizers"><h2>Momentum, Adagrad, RMSProp, Adadelta and Adam</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Momentum:</strong> maintains a moving accumulation of past gradients. Consistent directions gain speed; noisy changes are smoothed.</li><li><strong>Adagrad:</strong> accumulates squared gradients per parameter, shrinking the effective learning rate for parameters that have received large gradients. It can become too conservative.</li><li><strong>RMSProp:</strong> uses an exponentially weighted average of squared gradients, so the accumulator does not grow without bound as in basic Adagrad.</li><li><strong>Adadelta:</strong> adapts updates using running statistics of gradients and updates, reducing dependence on a manually chosen global learning-rate scale in its common formulation.</li><li><strong>Adam:</strong> combines moving averages of gradients (first moment) and squared gradients (second moment), with bias correction. It is a widely used default.</li></ul><p>Adaptive methods do not guarantee the best result for every task. Compare validation performance and tune learning rate and other settings.</p>
</section>
<section id="pytorch"><h2>Reading an optimizer training step</h2>
<pre><code>optimizer.zero_grad()  # clear gradients from previous batch
pred = model(x_batch)
loss = loss_fn(pred, y_batch)
loss.backward()        # compute gradients
optimizer.step()       # update parameters</code></pre><p><code>loss.backward()</code> computes gradients; it does not itself change model parameters. <code>optimizer.step()</code> applies an update using those gradients. If the optimizer is never stepped, parameters do not change through gradient descent.</p>
</section>
<section id="questions"><h2>Week 5 questions and solutions</h2>
<p>Try each question first. These are original exam-style questions based on the supplied optimization materials, not official GA questions.</p><details class="question"><summary>Q1. For L(w)=w², w=4 and learning rate 0.25, what is w after one gradient-descent update?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2</p><p>The derivative is 2w=8. Apply w_new=4−0.25×8=2.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q2. A training set has 1,200 examples and batch size 100. With the final batch retained, how many updates occur in 5 epochs?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> 60 updates</p><p>There are 1,200/100=12 mini-batches per epoch. Across 5 epochs: 12×5=60.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q3. What happens if the learning rate is much too large?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Updates may overshoot, oscillate or diverge.</p><p>The update multiplies the gradient by the learning rate. A very large step can jump past a minimum instead of approaching it.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q4. Which optimizer combines estimates of the first and second moments of gradients?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Adam</p><p>Adam tracks an exponential moving average of gradients and of squared gradients, then uses bias-corrected estimates.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q5. Why can momentum speed up optimization?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> It accumulates past gradients in consistent directions.</p><p>Repeated gradients pointing similarly reinforce the velocity, while changing/noisy directions are smoothed.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q6. Which method has the noisiest update estimate: full-batch GD, mini-batch size 64, or SGD with batch size 1?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> SGD with batch size 1</p><p>One sample gives a high-variance estimate of the full-data gradient. Larger batches generally reduce gradient noise.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q7. In PyTorch, does loss.backward() update the weights?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No</p><p>It calculates gradients and stores them in parameter .grad fields. The optimizer updates weights when optimizer.step() runs.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q8. What is the main drawback of basic Adagrad on long training runs?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Its effective learning rate can become very small.</p><p>Adagrad continually accumulates squared gradients; the denominator can grow over time and make later updates tiny.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q9. The gradient is −3, current weight is 2 and learning rate is 0.1. What is the new weight?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> 2.3</p><p>w_new=2−0.1(−3)=2+0.3=2.3. A negative gradient makes the parameter increase.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q10. True or false: doubling the learning rate always halves the number of epochs needed.</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> False</p><p>Training dynamics are nonlinear; a larger step can help, do little, or destabilize training. It does not guarantee fewer epochs.</p></div></details>
</section>
<section id="extra-question-bank-week5">
  <h2>Additional Week 5 Question Bank: Optimization Strategies</h2>
  <p>These original exam-style questions focus on gradient descent, batch size, momentum and adaptive optimizers.</p>

  <details class="question">
    <summary>Q1. The loss is L(w) = (w − 3)². If w = 1 and the learning rate is 0.1, what is w after one gradient-descent update?</summary>
    <div class="answer">
      <p><strong>Options:</strong> A. 0.6 · B. 1.4 · C. 2.0 · D. 3.0</p>
      <p><strong>Answer:</strong> B. 1.4</p>
      <p>The derivative is dL/dw = 2(w − 3). At w = 1, the gradient is −4. Therefore, w_new = 1 − 0.1(−4) = 1.4.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="question">
    <summary>Q2. A training set contains 2,000 examples and uses a mini-batch size of 128. If the final incomplete batch is retained, how many updates occur per epoch?</summary>
    <div class="answer">
      <p><strong>Options:</strong> A. 15 · B. 16 · C. 128 · D. 2,000</p>
      <p><strong>Answer:</strong> B. 16</p>
      <p>There are 15 complete batches, covering 1,920 examples, and one final batch containing 80 examples. Thus, the total is 16 updates per epoch.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="question">
    <summary>Q3. Which statement about momentum is most accurate?</summary>
    <div class="answer">
      <p><strong>Options:</strong> A. It removes the need for gradients · B. It accumulates information from previous gradients to smooth and accelerate updates · C. It always finds the global minimum · D. It freezes the model parameters after one epoch</p>
      <p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p>
      <p>Momentum maintains a running velocity influenced by earlier gradients. Consistent directions can build speed, while changing directions may be smoothed. It does not guarantee finding the global minimum.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="question">
    <summary>Q4. Which optimizer maintains moving averages related to both gradients and squared gradients?</summary>
    <div class="answer">
      <p><strong>Options:</strong> A. Adam · B. Plain batch gradient descent only · C. Max pooling · D. Dropout</p>
      <p><strong>Answer:</strong> A. Adam</p>
      <p>Adam tracks first-moment and second-moment estimates of gradients, with bias correction in its standard formulation. This provides momentum-like behaviour and adaptive parameter-wise scaling.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="question">
    <summary>Q5. What is a common drawback of Adagrad during long training runs?</summary>
    <div class="answer">
      <p><strong>Options:</strong> A. It never uses gradients · B. Its accumulated squared gradients can cause the effective learning rate to become very small · C. It only works with image data · D. It always increases the learning rate</p>
      <p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p>
      <p>Adagrad continually accumulates squared gradients in its denominator. As that accumulator grows, effective step sizes may shrink significantly, making later learning slow.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="question">
    <summary>Q6. In a standard PyTorch training step, what is the main purpose of <code>optimizer.zero_grad()</code>?</summary>
    <div class="answer">
      <p><strong>Options:</strong> A. To reset model weights to random values · B. To clear gradients accumulated from previous backward passes · C. To calculate validation accuracy · D. To update the learning-rate schedule automatically</p>
      <p><strong>Answer:</strong> B</p>
      <p>PyTorch accumulates gradients by default. Clearing them before the next backward pass prevents unintended accumulation across batches in an ordinary training loop. The optimizer updates parameters when <code>optimizer.step()</code> is called.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

