      <h2>1. The big picture: how a neural network learns</h2>

      <p>
        In Week 1, we learned that a neural network consists of neurons,
        weights, biases, activation functions, and layers. We also saw how
        to create tensors and build a simple model in PyTorch.
      </p>

      <p>
        Week 2 focuses on the learning process. A network starts with
        parameters that may be far from correct. It makes predictions,
        measures how wrong they are, calculates how its parameters should
        change, and updates those parameters repeatedly.
      </p>

      <h3>The complete learning cycle</h3>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Input:</strong> Supply a batch of examples to the model.</li>
        <li><strong>Forward pass:</strong> Calculate predictions from the current weights and biases.</li>
        <li><strong>Loss calculation:</strong> Compare predictions with the correct targets.</li>
        <li><strong>Backward pass:</strong> Calculate gradients that describe how the loss changes with each parameter.</li>
        <li><strong>Optimizer step:</strong> Update parameters using those gradients.</li>
        <li><strong>Repeat:</strong> Continue for multiple batches and epochs.</li>
        <li><strong>Validation:</strong> Evaluate performance on data not used to update the parameters.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Important distinction:</strong> The forward pass makes predictions.
        The backward pass calculates gradients. The optimizer updates parameters.
        These are three separate operations.
      </div>

      <h3>Essential terminology</h3>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Parameter</td><td>A value learned by the model, such as a weight or bias.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Forward pass</td><td>Computing predictions from inputs and parameters.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Target</td><td>The correct output associated with an input.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Loss</td><td>A numerical measure of prediction error.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Gradient</td><td>The derivative of the loss with respect to a parameter.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Learning rate</td><td>A factor controlling the size of a gradient-based update.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Epoch</td><td>One complete pass through the training dataset.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Batch</td><td>A subset of training examples processed together.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Validation</td><td>Evaluating a model on data that is not used for parameter updates.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section id="uat">
      
      <h2>2. Universal Approximation Theorem</h2>

      <h3>2.1 What is the theorem?</h3>

      <p>
        The Universal Approximation Theorem describes the expressive power
        of neural networks. Under suitable conditions, a feedforward neural
        network with a hidden layer containing enough neurons and a suitable
        nonlinear activation function can approximate a continuous function
        on a compact domain as closely as desired.
      </p>

      <p>
        In simpler words, a neural network can represent a wide variety of
        complicated input-output relationships, even when those relationships
        are not straight lines.
      </p>

      <h3>2.2 Why are hidden layers useful?</h3>

      <p>
        Consider a simple linear model:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(y = wx+b\)
      </p>

      <p>
        This model can represent a straight-line relationship between a
        single input and output. Many real-world relationships are more
        complicated: customer behaviour, image patterns, and interactions
        among variables may not follow a straight line.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hidden layers allow a network to combine intermediate features.
        Nonlinear activation functions allow the network to represent
        nonlinear relationships.
      </p>

      <h3>2.3 Why does nonlinearity matter?</h3>

      <p>
        Suppose every layer performs only a linear transformation:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(h_1=W_1x+b_1\)
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(h_2=W_2h_1+b_2\)
      </p>

      <p>
        Substituting the first equation into the second gives:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(h_2=W_2(W_1x+b_1)+b_2\)
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(h_2=(W_2W_1)x+(W_2b_1+b_2)\)
      </p>

      <p>
        This is still a linear transformation followed by a bias. Stacking
        linear layers without nonlinear activations does not provide the
        same expressive power as a nonlinear network.
      </p>

      <h3>2.4 Common nonlinear activation functions</h3>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Activation</th><th>Formula</th><th>Typical behaviour</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Sigmoid</td>
            <td>\(\sigma(x)=\frac{1}{1+e^{-x}}\)</td>
            <td>Maps values to the interval (0, 1).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Tanh</td>
            <td>\(\tanh(x)\)</td>
            <td>Maps values to the interval (-1, 1).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>ReLU</td>
            <td>\(\max(0,x)\)</td>
            <td>Returns zero for negative inputs and the input itself for positive inputs.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Leaky ReLU</td>
            <td>\(\max(\alpha x,x)\)</td>
            <td>Allows a small negative output when the input is negative.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>2.5 What the theorem does not guarantee</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>It does not guarantee that a small network will approximate every function well.</li>
        <li>It does not guarantee that training will find the right parameters.</li>
        <li>It does not guarantee good performance on unseen data.</li>
        <li>It does not say that one particular architecture is best for every task.</li>
        <li>It does not mean that every function can be represented exactly by a finite network.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>GA concept:</strong> The theorem concerns what a network can
        approximate under suitable conditions. It is not a guarantee about
        how quickly, or whether, an optimization algorithm will learn that
        approximation.
      </div>

      <h3>Original GA-style example</h3>

      <p><strong>Question:</strong> Which change is most important for allowing a multilayer feedforward network to represent nonlinear relationships?</p>

      <ol type="A">
        <li>Removing every bias from the network.</li>
        <li>Adding nonlinear activation functions between linear transformations.</li>
        <li>Using only one neuron for every problem.</li>
        <li>Setting all weights permanently to zero.</li>
      </ol>

      <details>
        <summary>Show answer and explanation</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          <strong>Answer: B.</strong> Nonlinear activations allow the
          composition of layers to represent nonlinear relationships.
          Additional linear layers alone can still collapse into one linear
          transformation.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section id="forward-pass">
      <h2>3. Forward pass through a neural network</h2>

      <h3>3.1 What happens during a forward pass?</h3>

      <p>
        The forward pass takes input data and passes it through the network
        layer by layer. Each layer performs a weighted sum, adds a bias,
        and may apply an activation function.
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(z=Wx+b\)
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(a=f(z)\)
      </p>

      <p>Here:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>\(x\) is the input vector.</li>
        <li>\(W\) is the weight matrix.</li>
        <li>\(b\) is the bias vector.</li>
        <li>\(z\) is the result before activation.</li>
        <li>\(f\) is the activation function.</li>
        <li>\(a\) is the activation or output of that layer.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>3.2 Calculating one neuron by hand</h3>

      <p>Suppose a neuron receives two inputs:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(x_1=2,\quad x_2=3\)
      </p>

      <p>Its weights and bias are:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(w_1=0.5,\quad w_2=-1,\quad b=1\)
      </p>

      <p>First calculate the weighted sum:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(z=w_1x_1+w_2x_2+b\)
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(z=(0.5)(2)+(-1)(3)+1=-1\)
      </p>

      <p>With ReLU activation:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(a=\max(0,-1)=0\)
      </p>

      <p>
        The neuron's output is therefore zero. Notice that the bias is
        included before the activation function.
      </p>

      <h3>3.3 Forward pass through two layers</h3>

      <p>
        Suppose a network has one input, two hidden neurons, and one output
        neuron. Use the following parameters:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>Input: \(x=2\)</li>
        <li>Hidden weights: \(w_1=1,\ w_2=-1\)</li>
        <li>Hidden biases: \(b_1=0,\ b_2=1\)</li>
        <li>Hidden activation: ReLU</li>
        <li>Output weights: \(v_1=2,\ v_2=3\)</li>
        <li>Output bias: \(c=1\)</li>
      </ul>

      <p>First hidden neuron:</p>
      <p class="eq">\(z_1=(1)(2)+0=2\)</p>
      <p class="eq">\(h_1=\operatorname{ReLU}(2)=2\)</p>

      <p>Second hidden neuron:</p>
      <p class="eq">\(z_2=(-1)(2)+1=-1\)</p>
      <p class="eq">\(h_2=\operatorname{ReLU}(-1)=0\)</p>

      <p>Output neuron, assuming a linear output:</p>
      <p class="eq">\(y=(2)(h_1)+(3)(h_2)+1\)</p>
      <p class="eq">\(y=(2)(2)+(3)(0)+1=5\)</p>

      <p>
        The final output is 5. If the output layer instead used a sigmoid,
        the value before sigmoid would still be 5, but the final output
        would be \(\sigma(5)\), approximately 0.993.
      </p>

      <h3>3.4 Matrix form</h3>

      <p>
        When a layer has many neurons, matrix multiplication performs all
        weighted sums efficiently.
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(z=Wx+b\)
      </p>

      <p>
        If the input has 4 features and the layer has 3 neurons, then
        \(x\) has shape \((4,)\), \(W\) can have shape \((3,4)\), and
        \(b\) has shape \((3,)\). The resulting \(z\) has shape \((3,)\).
      </p>

      <p>
        With a batch of 8 examples, the input often has shape \((8,4)\).
        In PyTorch, a linear layer with 4 input features and 3 output
        features uses weights of shape \((3,4)\) and produces an output
        of shape \((8,3)\).
      </p>

      <h3>3.5 A forward pass in PyTorch</h3>

      <pre><code>import torch
import torch.nn as nn

model = nn.Sequential(
    nn.Linear(4, 8),
    nn.ReLU(),
    nn.Linear(8, 3)
)

x = torch.randn(5, 4)
output = model(x)

print(output.shape)  # torch.Size([5, 3])</code></pre>

      <p>
        Here, the model receives five examples, each containing four
        features, and produces three output values per example.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Exam tip:</strong> For a forward-pass numerical question,
        calculate one layer at a time. Do not apply the next layer until
        you have calculated the previous layer's activation.
      </div>

      <h3>Original GA-style example</h3>

      <p><strong>Question:</strong> A neuron has inputs \(x_1=3,\ x_2=2\), weights \(w_1=2,\ w_2=-1\), bias \(b=-2\), and ReLU activation. What is its output?</p>

      <ol type="A">
        <li>-2</li>
        <li>0</li>
        <li>2</li>
        <li>4</li>
      </ol>

      <details>
        <summary>Show calculation</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          \(z=2(3)+(-1)(2)-2=2\). Applying ReLU gives
          \(\max(0,2)=2\). <strong>Answer: C.</strong>
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section id="loss">
      <h2>4. Loss functions</h2>

      <h3>4.1 What is a loss function?</h3>

      <p>
        A loss function measures how different a model's prediction is from
        the target. Training uses the loss to determine whether parameter
        updates are improving the model.
      </p>

      <p>
        A loss is a numerical value. A smaller loss usually indicates
        predictions that better match the targets according to that
        particular loss function. The best loss depends on the task.
      </p>

      <h3>4.2 Mean Squared Error (MSE)</h3>

      <p>
        Mean Squared Error is commonly used for regression, where the goal
        is to predict a numerical value.
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\operatorname{MSE}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}(y_i-\hat y_i)^2\)
      </p>

      <p>
        The error for each example is squared, and the squared errors are
        averaged. Squaring makes large errors contribute more heavily.
      </p>

      <h4>Worked example</h4>

      <p>Suppose the targets are \([3,5]\) and predictions are \([2,7]\).</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\operatorname{MSE}=\frac{(3-2)^2+(5-7)^2}{2}\)
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(=\frac{1+4}{2}=2.5\)
      </p>

      <p>The MSE is 2.5.</p>

      <pre><code>criterion = nn.MSELoss()
loss = criterion(predictions, targets)</code></pre>

      <h3>4.3 Binary cross-entropy (BCE)</h3>

      <p>
        Binary cross-entropy is used for binary classification, such as
        predicting whether a customer will leave a service.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let \(y\) be the binary target and \(p\) be the predicted probability
        of class 1. The binary cross-entropy for one example is:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(-[y\log(p)+(1-y)\log(1-p)]\)
      </p>

      <p>
        If the target is 1, the expression reduces to \(-\log(p)\).
        Predicting a probability close to 1 gives a small loss; predicting
        a probability close to 0 gives a large loss.
      </p>

      <p>
        If the target is 0, the expression reduces to \(-\log(1-p)\).
        The loss is small when the predicted probability is close to 0.
      </p>

      <pre><code># Model returns logits (not sigmoid probabilities)
criterion = nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss()
logits = model(x)
loss = criterion(logits, targets.float())</code></pre>

      <p>
        <code>BCEWithLogitsLoss</code> combines sigmoid and binary
        cross-entropy in a numerically stable operation. When using this
        loss, the model should normally return logits rather than applying
        a sigmoid inside the model.
      </p>

      <h3>4.4 Categorical cross-entropy</h3>

      <p>
        For a mutually exclusive multiclass classification task, such as
        classifying an image into one of ten categories, a common choice is
        categorical cross-entropy.
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(L=-\sum_{k=1}^{C}y_k\log(p_k)\)
      </p>

      <p>
        Here \(C\) is the number of classes, \(y_k\) is the target
        indicator for class \(k\), and \(p_k\) is the predicted probability
        for that class. With one-hot targets, only the correct class
        contributes to the sum.
      </p>

      <p>
        In PyTorch, <code>nn.CrossEntropyLoss</code> normally receives
        unnormalized class scores called logits. It combines the
        log-softmax operation and negative log-likelihood loss.
      </p>

      <pre><code>criterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss()
logits = model(images)  # [batch_size, number_of_classes]
loss = criterion(logits, labels)  # integer class indices</code></pre>

      <p>
        For a batch of images with 10 possible classes, the logits generally
        have shape \((B,10)\), while the class-index labels generally have
        shape \((B,)\).
      </p>

      <h3>4.5 Which loss should you choose?</h3>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Task</th><th>Typical loss</th><th>Common target format</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Regression</td><td>MSE</td><td>Numerical values</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Binary classification</td><td>BCE or BCE with logits</td><td>0/1 values</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Single-label multiclass classification</td><td>Cross-entropy</td><td>Integer class indices</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Common mistake:</strong> Do not confuse the final predicted
        class with the values passed to a loss function. For
        <code>CrossEntropyLoss</code>, pass logits, not argmax class indices
        or already-softmaxed probabilities.
      </div>

      <h3>Original GA-style example</h3>

      <p><strong>Question:</strong> A neural network predicts the category of each image among ten mutually exclusive categories. Which loss is a standard choice?</p>

      <ol type="A">
        <li>Mean Squared Error in every case</li>
        <li>Binary cross-entropy for ten independent binary labels in every case</li>
        <li>Cross-entropy for single-label multiclass classification</li>
        <li>No loss function is needed</li>
      </ol>

      <details>
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          <strong>Answer: C.</strong> For one correct class among ten
          mutually exclusive classes, <code>nn.CrossEntropyLoss</code> is
          a standard choice.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section id="gradient-descent">
      <h2>5. Gradient descent</h2>

      <h3>5.1 What is a gradient?</h3>

      <p>
        A gradient describes how a function changes with respect to its
        input parameters. For a model with many parameters, the gradient
        contains a derivative for every parameter.
      </p>

      <p>
        If the loss is \(L\) and a parameter is \(w\), then
        \(\frac{\partial L}{\partial w}\) describes the local rate of
        change in loss as \(w\) changes.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>A positive gradient means increasing the parameter slightly would increase the loss locally.</li>
        <li>A negative gradient means increasing the parameter slightly would decrease the loss locally.</li>
        <li>A gradient close to zero means the loss changes very little locally in that direction.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>5.2 The gradient descent update rule</h3>

      <p class="eq">
        \(w_{\text{new}}=w_{\text{old}}-\eta\frac{\partial L}{\partial w}\)
      </p>

      <p>Here:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>\(w_{\text{old}}\) is the current parameter.</li>
        <li>\(\eta\) is the learning rate.</li>
        <li>\(\frac{\partial L}{\partial w}\) is the gradient of the loss with respect to the parameter.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The minus sign means the update moves in the direction opposite
        to the gradient, which is the local direction of steepest decrease
        for a differentiable loss.
      </p>

      <h3>5.3 Numerical example</h3>

      <p>Suppose:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(w_{\text{old}}=2,\quad \eta=0.1,\quad
        \frac{\partial L}{\partial w}=4\)
      </p>

      <p>Then:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(w_{\text{new}}=2-(0.1)(4)=1.6\)
      </p>

      <p>
        The updated weight is 1.6. The positive gradient causes the
        parameter to decrease in this step.
      </p>

      <h3>5.4 What happens when the learning rate changes?</h3>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Learning rate</th><th>Possible effect</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Too small</td><td>Training may progress very slowly.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reasonable</td><td>Updates may make useful progress toward lower loss.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Too large</td><td>Updates may overshoot, oscillate, or diverge.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        A larger learning rate does not automatically mean a better model.
        The best value depends on the model, data, loss surface, and
        optimizer.
      </p>

      <h3>5.5 Batch, stochastic, and mini-batch gradient descent</h3>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Method</th><th>How examples are used</th><th>Practical characteristic</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Batch gradient descent</td>
            <td>Uses the entire training dataset for one gradient calculation.</td>
            <td>Can be expensive for large datasets.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Stochastic gradient descent</td>
            <td>Uses one example per update.</td>
            <td>Frequent, noisy updates.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mini-batch gradient descent</td>
            <td>Uses a small batch of examples per update.</td>
            <td>Common in deep learning; balances computational efficiency and update frequency.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        If a dataset contains 1,000 examples and the batch size is 100,
        one epoch normally consists of 10 mini-batches, assuming all
        examples are used and the last batch is full.
      </p>

      <h3>5.6 Optimizers in PyTorch</h3>

      <pre><code>optimizer = torch.optim.SGD(model.parameters(), lr=0.01)

optimizer.zero_grad()  # Clear previous gradients
predictions = model(x)  # Forward pass
loss = criterion(predictions, targets)
loss.backward()  # Calculate gradients
optimizer.step()  # Update parameters</code></pre>

      <p>
        PyTorch optimizers implement the parameter update rules. SGD is
        a common gradient-based optimizer; Adam is another widely used
        optimizer that adapts updates using running estimates of gradients
        and squared gradients.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Order matters:</strong> Calculate the loss, call
        <code>backward()</code> to compute gradients, and then call
        <code>step()</code> to update the parameters. Clear old gradients
        before accumulating the next batch's gradients.
      </div>

      <h3>Original GA-style example</h3>

      <p><strong>Question:</strong> A weight is 0.8, the learning rate is 0.05, and its gradient is -2. What is the new weight after one standard gradient descent update?</p>

      <details>
        <summary>Show calculation</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          \(w_{\text{new}}=0.8-(0.05)(-2)=0.8+0.1=0.9\).
          <strong>Answer: 0.9.</strong>
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section id="churn">
      <h2>6. Customer churn prediction with a neural network</h2>

      <h3>6.1 What is customer churn?</h3>

      <p>
        Customer churn occurs when a customer stops using a service or
        leaves a business. A churn prediction model estimates the likelihood
        that a customer will leave, based on information such as usage,
        tenure, subscription type, and billing information.
      </p>

      <p>
        If the target indicates whether a customer churned, the task is
        typically a binary classification problem.
      </p>

      <h3>6.2 Understand the dataset</h3>

      <p>A tabular churn dataset may contain:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Numerical features: tenure, monthly charges, number of support calls.</li>
        <li>Categorical features: contract type, payment method, service plan.</li>
        <li>Target: whether the customer churned, often encoded as 0 or 1.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The exact columns and preprocessing steps depend on the dataset
        used in the lecture. The following is a general workflow, not a
        claim about a particular course dataset.
      </p>

      <h3>6.3 Data preprocessing</h3>

      <ol>
        <li>Load the dataset and inspect its columns, data types, and missing values.</li>
        <li>Separate input features from the target.</li>
        <li>Encode categorical features into numerical representations.</li>
        <li>Handle missing values according to a consistent policy.</li>
        <li>Scale numerical features when appropriate.</li>
        <li>Split the data into training and validation sets.</li>
        <li>Convert processed features and targets to suitable tensor types.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        A neural network operates on numerical tensors. Text categories
        such as "monthly" and "annual" cannot be passed directly into an
        ordinary linear layer without encoding.
      </p>

      <h3>6.4 Why feature scaling can help</h3>

      <p>
        Suppose one feature represents customer tenure between 0 and 10,
        while another represents annual charges between 0 and 100,000.
        Their numerical scales differ considerably.
      </p>

      <p>
        Scaling can make optimization easier by preventing some numerical
        features from dominating simply because of their units. A common
        standardization is:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(x_{\text{scaled}}=\frac{x-\mu}{\sigma}\)
      </p>

      <p>
        Estimate scaling statistics from the training data and apply those
        same statistics to the validation data. Using validation data to
        fit preprocessing parameters can leak information into model
        development.
      </p>

      <h3>6.5 Package imports</h3>

      <pre><code>import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
import torch
import torch.nn as nn
from torch.utils.data import DataLoader, TensorDataset
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler</code></pre>

      <p>
        These packages support data manipulation, tensor operations,
        neural network construction, mini-batch loading, dataset splitting,
        and feature scaling. Exact imports depend on the implementation.
      </p>

      <h3>6.6 Define a neural network</h3>

      <p>
        Suppose the processed dataset contains \(d\) input features.
        A simple model can map those features to a hidden representation
        and then produce one output logit.
      </p>

      <pre><code>class ChurnMLP(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, input_dim):
        super().__init__()

        self.network = nn.Sequential(
            nn.Linear(input_dim, 32),
            nn.ReLU(),
            nn.Linear(32, 16),
            nn.ReLU(),
            nn.Linear(16, 1)
        )

    def forward(self, x):
        return self.network(x)</code></pre>

      <p>
        The hidden layers learn intermediate combinations of the input
        features. The final layer produces one logit for each example.
        A sigmoid can convert that logit into a probability when needed.
      </p>

      <h3>6.7 Weight initialization</h3>

      <p>
        Training typically starts with initialized weights and biases.
        If every neuron in the same layer begins with identical weights,
        the neurons can receive identical gradients and fail to learn
        different features.
      </p>

      <p>
        Randomized initialization helps break this symmetry. The
        initialization method should also be appropriate for the
        activation function and network architecture.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Xavier/Glorot initialization:</strong> Often used with activations such as tanh, with variants depending on the layer.</li>
        <li><strong>Kaiming/He initialization:</strong> Designed with ReLU-like activations in mind.</li>
        <li><strong>Bias initialization:</strong> Often begins at zero, although some models use other choices.</li>
      </ul>

      <pre><code>model = ChurnMLP(input_dim)

for layer in model.modules():
    if isinstance(layer, nn.Linear):
        nn.init.kaiming_uniform_(
            layer.weight,
            nonlinearity="relu"
        )
        nn.init.zeros_(layer.bias)</code></pre>

      <p>
        This example applies Kaiming initialization to all linear layers.
        For a final output layer, other initialization choices may also
        be appropriate. Initialization is not the same as the later
        gradient-based learning process.
      </p>

      <h3>6.8 Training and validation setup</h3>

      <p>
        The training set is used to calculate gradients and update
        parameters. The validation set is used to monitor how well the
        current model performs on held-out examples.
      </p>

      <pre><code>train_dataset = TensorDataset(X_train, y_train)
val_dataset = TensorDataset(X_val, y_val)

train_loader = DataLoader(
    train_dataset,
    batch_size=64,
    shuffle=True
)

val_loader = DataLoader(
    val_dataset,
    batch_size=64,
    shuffle=False
)</code></pre>

      <p>
        Shuffling the training examples helps avoid relying on a fixed
        order of examples. Validation data is usually not shuffled because
        its order does not need to be randomized for evaluation.
      </p>

      <h3>6.9 Training loop</h3>

      <pre><code>model = ChurnMLP(X_train.shape[1])
criterion = nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss()
optimizer = torch.optim.Adam(
    model.parameters(),
    lr=0.001
)

for epoch in range(20):
    model.train()
    total_loss = 0.0

    for features, targets in train_loader:
        targets = targets.float().view(-1, 1)

        optimizer.zero_grad()

        logits = model(features)
        loss = criterion(logits, targets)

        loss.backward()
        optimizer.step()

        total_loss += loss.item() * features.size(0)

    average_loss = total_loss / len(train_loader.dataset)
    print(f"Epoch {epoch + 1}: loss = {average_loss:.4f}")</code></pre>

      <p>
        This example assumes that the feature tensors and target tensors
        have already been prepared with compatible shapes and types.
        The targets are converted to floating-point values with shape
        \((B,1)\) to match the output logits.
      </p>

      <h3>6.10 Model training versus validation</h3>

      <p>
        The model should be put into training mode during training and
        evaluation mode during validation. These modes affect layers such
        as dropout and batch normalization when present.
      </p>

      <pre><code>model.train()  # Training mode
# Forward pass, loss, backward pass, optimizer update

model.eval()   # Evaluation mode
with torch.no_grad():
    # Forward pass and evaluation only
    pass</code></pre>

      <p>
        <code>model.eval()</code> does not disable gradients by itself.
        <code>torch.no_grad()</code> prevents gradient tracking in the
        enclosed computation. These are separate operations.
      </p>

      <h3>6.11 Converting logits to churn predictions</h3>

      <p>
        For a binary classifier, sigmoid converts a logit \(z\) into a
        probability:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(p=\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}\)
      </p>

      <p>
        A threshold such as 0.5 can be used to produce a binary prediction:
        classify as class 1 when \(p\geq0.5\). The threshold can be changed
        depending on the cost of false positives and false negatives.
      </p>

      <pre><code>probabilities = torch.sigmoid(logits)
predictions = (probabilities >= 0.5).int()</code></pre>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>GA focus:</strong> Be ready to identify preprocessing steps,
        tensor shapes, loss-function choice, training-loop order,
        parameter initialization, and the difference between training
        and validation.
      </div>

      <h3>Original GA-style example</h3>

      <p><strong>Question:</strong> A binary churn model returns logits of shape \((32,1)\). Its target tensor contains 32 labels encoded as 0 or 1. Which target transformation is compatible with the example training code above?</p>

      <ol type="A">
        <li>Convert the targets to floating-point values and reshape to \((32,1)\).</li>
        <li>Convert the targets to text strings.</li>
        <li>Convert the targets to shape \((32,10)\) without changing the model.</li>
        <li>Apply argmax to the target labels.</li>
      </ol>

      <details>
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          <strong>Answer: A.</strong> <code>BCEWithLogitsLoss</code> expects
          floating-point targets compatible with the logits' shape. Here
          both have shape \((32,1)\).
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section id="backprop">
      <h2>7. Backpropagation</h2>

      <h3>7.1 What is backpropagation?</h3>

      <p>
        Backpropagation is an efficient method for computing derivatives
        of a loss with respect to the parameters of a neural network. It
        applies the chain rule from the output of the network back through
        the preceding computations.
      </p>

      <p>
        The forward pass calculates intermediate values. Backpropagation
        uses those values to calculate gradients. An optimizer then uses
        the gradients to update the parameters.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        Backpropagation calculates gradients; it does not, by itself,
        update the weights. The optimizer performs the update.
      </div>

      <h3>7.2 The chain rule</h3>

      <p>
        If \(y=f(u)\) and \(u=g(x)\), then \(y=f(g(x))\). The chain rule
        gives:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{dy}{du}\frac{du}{dx}\)
      </p>

      <p>
        The derivative of the outer function is multiplied by the
        derivative of the inner function. This principle allows us to
        calculate gradients through multiple connected operations.
      </p>

      <h3>7.3 Backpropagation through a simple scalar chain</h3>

      <p>Consider the computation:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(x=2,\quad a=3x,\quad y=a^2\)
      </p>

      <p>First calculate the forward values:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(a=3(2)=6,\quad y=6^2=36\)
      </p>

      <p>Now calculate the derivatives:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\frac{dy}{da}=2a=12\)
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\frac{da}{dx}=3\)
      </p>

      <p>Apply the chain rule:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{dy}{da}\frac{da}{dx}=12(3)=36\)
      </p>

      <p>
        The derivative of the final output with respect to \(x\) is 36.
      </p>

      <h3>7.4 A second scalar example involving loss</h3>

      <p>Suppose a model predicts:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\hat y=wx,\quad L=\frac{1}{2}(\hat y-y)^2\)
      </p>

      <p>
        To calculate the gradient with respect to \(w\), apply the chain
        rule:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\frac{\partial L}{\partial w}
        =\frac{\partial L}{\partial\hat y}
        \frac{\partial\hat y}{\partial w}\)
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\frac{\partial L}{\partial\hat y}=\hat y-y\)
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\frac{\partial\hat y}{\partial w}=x\)
      </p>

      <p>Therefore:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\frac{\partial L}{\partial w}=(\hat y-y)x\)
      </p>

      <p>
        If \(x=2\), \(w=3\), and \(y=4\), then \(\hat y=6\), so:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\frac{\partial L}{\partial w}=(6-4)(2)=4\)
      </p>

      <h3>7.5 Backpropagation through an MLP</h3>

      <p>
        A multilayer perceptron (MLP) consists of connected layers. During
        the forward pass, each layer's output becomes the next layer's
        input. During backpropagation, gradients flow in the reverse
        direction.
      </p>

      <p>For a two-layer network:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(z_1=W_1x+b_1,\quad a_1=f(z_1)\)
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(z_2=W_2a_1+b_2,\quad \hat y=g(z_2)\)
      </p>

      <p>
        The loss depends on \(\hat y\). Backpropagation first calculates
        how the loss changes with respect to the output layer, then
        propagates that information through the hidden layer.
      </p>

      <p>
        For example, the gradient with respect to the second layer's
        weights depends on the output-layer error and the hidden
        activations. The gradient with respect to the first layer's
        weights also depends on how the hidden activations affect the
        output.
      </p>

      <h3>7.6 Activation derivatives</h3>

      <p>
        Activation functions contribute derivatives to the chain rule.
        For ReLU, away from zero:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(f'(z)=
        \begin{cases}
        0,&z&lt;0\\
        1,&z&gt;0
        \end{cases}\)
      </p>

      <p>
        If a ReLU neuron has a negative pre-activation, its local
        derivative is zero. This can block gradient flow through that
        neuron for that input.
      </p>

      <p>
        For sigmoid:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\sigma'(z)=\sigma(z)(1-\sigma(z))\)
      </p>

      <p>
        The sigmoid derivative becomes small when its output is close
        to 0 or 1, which can contribute to vanishing gradients in deep
        networks.
      </p>

      <h3>7.7 Backpropagation in PyTorch</h3>

      <pre><code>x = torch.tensor([2.0], requires_grad=True)

a = 3 * x
y = a ** 2

y.backward()

print(x.grad)  # tensor([36.])</code></pre>

      <p>
        Setting <code>requires_grad=True</code> allows PyTorch to track
        operations involving the tensor. Calling <code>backward()</code>
        computes the gradient of the scalar output with respect to
        tracked inputs.
      </p>

      <p>
        In ordinary model training, parameters already have gradient
        tracking enabled. Their gradients can be accessed through
        <code>parameter.grad</code> after backpropagation.
      </p>

      <h3>Original GA-style example</h3>

      <p><strong>Question:</strong> Let \(u=2x\), \(y=u^3\), and \(x=2\). What is \(\frac{dy}{dx}\) at this value?</p>

      <details>
        <summary>Show calculation</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          By the chain rule,
          \(\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{dy}{du}\frac{du}{dx}=3u^2(2)\).
          Since \(u=2(2)=4\), the derivative is
          \(3(16)(2)=96\).
          <strong>Answer: 96.</strong>
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section id="xor">
      <h2>8. The XOR problem</h2>

      <h3>8.1 What is XOR?</h3>

      <p>
        XOR stands for exclusive OR. For two binary inputs, the output is
        1 when the inputs are different and 0 when they are the same.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>\(x_1\)</th><th>\(x_2\)</th><th>\(x_1\operatorname{XOR}x_2\)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>8.2 Why is XOR a problem for a single-layer model?</h3>

      <p>
        A single neuron with a linear decision boundary cannot separate
        the XOR examples into the correct two classes. The positive
        examples are \((0,1)\) and \((1,0)\), while the negative examples
        are \((0,0)\) and \((1,1)\).
      </p>

      <p>
        No single straight line can place both positive examples on one
        side and both negative examples on the other. XOR is therefore
        not linearly separable.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Important:</strong> The limitation concerns a single
        linear decision boundary. A network with hidden neurons and
        nonlinear activations can combine intermediate representations
        to solve XOR.
      </div>

      <h3>8.3 How a hidden layer helps</h3>

      <p>
        A small network can create intermediate features that respond to
        different input combinations. A later layer combines those
        features to produce the XOR output.
      </p>

      <p>
        One way to understand the idea is that hidden neurons can form
        separate decision regions. The output neuron then combines these
        regions. The key is that the network is no longer restricted to
        one linear decision boundary.
      </p>

      <h3>8.4 A threshold-unit illustration</h3>

      <p>
        For conceptual understanding, define a threshold unit that returns
        1 when its input is greater than zero and 0 otherwise.
      </p>

      <p>Consider two hidden units:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(h_1=\operatorname{step}(x_1+x_2-0.5)\)
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(h_2=\operatorname{step}(x_1+x_2-1.5)\)
      </p>

      <p>
        The first unit detects whether at least one input is 1. The second
        detects whether both inputs are 1.
      </p>

      <p>Then the XOR result can be expressed as:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(y=\operatorname{step}(h_1-2h_2-0.5)\)
      </p>

      <p>
        Check the four input combinations:
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>\(x_1\)</th><th>\(x_2\)</th><th>\(h_1\)</th><th>\(h_2\)</th><th>Output</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        This is a threshold-unit illustration of the logic. A practical
        neural network trained using gradient descent commonly uses
        differentiable activations such as sigmoid or tanh instead of
        the hard step function.
      </p>

      <h3>8.5 Forward pass and backpropagation for XOR</h3>

      <p>
        The forward pass evaluates each hidden neuron, applies its
        activation, and then calculates the output. A loss function
        compares the output with the correct XOR label.
      </p>

      <p>
        Backpropagation calculates how the loss changes with respect to
        the hidden and output weights. Gradient-based optimization
        adjusts those parameters across training steps.
      </p>

      <p>
        With sigmoid activations, the forward calculation for each neuron
        follows:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(z=Wx+b,\quad a=\sigma(z)\)
      </p>

      <p>
        The output can then be compared with the target using a
        classification loss. The gradients propagate through the output
        layer and then the hidden layer.
      </p>

      <h3>Original GA-style example</h3>

      <p><strong>Question:</strong> Why can a single perceptron with a linear decision boundary not correctly classify the XOR truth table?</p>

      <ol type="A">
        <li>XOR requires the input values to be floating-point tensors.</li>
        <li>The XOR classes are not linearly separable.</li>
        <li>Gradient descent cannot update any weight in any network.</li>
        <li>XOR has more than ten output classes.</li>
      </ol>

      <details>
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          <strong>Answer: B.</strong> The two positive examples cannot
          be separated from the two negative examples by one straight
          line. Hidden layers with nonlinear activations can solve this
          limitation.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section id="fashion-overview">
      <h2>9. Fashion MNIST classification: overview and data processing</h2>

      <h3>9.1 What is Fashion MNIST?</h3>

      <p>
        Fashion MNIST is an image classification dataset containing
        grayscale images of fashion items. Each image is 28 by 28 pixels
        and belongs to one of ten classes.
      </p>

      <p>The ten classes are:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>T-shirt/top</li>
        <li>Trouser</li>
        <li>Pullover</li>
        <li>Dress</li>
        <li>Coat</li>
        <li>Sandal</li>
        <li>Shirt</li>
        <li>Sneaker</li>
        <li>Bag</li>
        <li>Ankle boot</li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Unlike customer churn prediction, which usually uses tabular
        features, Fashion MNIST uses image pixels as input features.
      </p>

      <h3>9.2 Image representation</h3>

      <p>
        A grayscale image contains 28 × 28 = 784 pixel values. A simple
        MLP can flatten these pixel values into a vector of length 784.
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(28\times28=784\)
      </p>

      <p>
        When using a batch of \(B\) images, the original image tensor
        typically has shape \((B,1,28,28)\), where the second dimension
        is the number of channels.
      </p>

      <p>
        After flattening, the shape becomes \((B,784)\). Flattening
        changes the shape, not the number of pixel values in each image.
      </p>

      <h3>9.3 Download and transform the dataset</h3>

      <pre><code>from torchvision import datasets, transforms
from torch.utils.data import DataLoader

transform = transforms.ToTensor()

train_dataset = datasets.FashionMNIST(
    root="./data",
    train=True,
    download=True,
    transform=transform
)

test_dataset = datasets.FashionMNIST(
    root="./data",
    train=False,
    download=True,
    transform=transform
)

train_loader = DataLoader(
    train_dataset,
    batch_size=64,
    shuffle=True
)

test_loader = DataLoader(
    test_dataset,
    batch_size=64,
    shuffle=False
)</code></pre>

      <p>
        <code>transforms.ToTensor()</code> converts a supported image
        into a tensor and scales its pixel values to the range [0, 1].
        The precise preprocessing pipeline may vary depending on the
        course implementation.
      </p>

      <h3>9.4 Training, validation, and test sets</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Training set:</strong> Used to learn model parameters.</li>
        <li><strong>Validation set:</strong> Used to monitor performance and guide model selection or hyperparameter tuning.</li>
        <li><strong>Test set:</strong> Used for a final evaluation after model choices have been made.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        In a general workflow, part of the training data may be reserved
        for validation. The official test set should not be repeatedly
        used to tune the model, because doing so can make test performance
        less representative of generalization.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        Fashion MNIST's test set is not automatically the same as a
        validation set. Use the exact split described by the course
        implementation when reproducing its results.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="fashion-model">
      <h2>10. Fashion MNIST MLP implementation in PyTorch</h2>

      <h3>10.1 Why use an MLP?</h3>

      <p>
        A multilayer perceptron treats each image as a vector of pixel
        values and learns combinations of those values. It is a useful
        model for understanding the complete classification pipeline.
      </p>

      <p>
        An MLP does not explicitly preserve the two-dimensional spatial
        structure in the way a convolutional neural network does. Even
        so, it can learn a useful mapping from flattened pixels to class
        scores.
      </p>

      <h3>10.2 Define the network</h3>

      <pre><code>import torch
import torch.nn as nn

class FashionMLP(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()

        self.network = nn.Sequential(
            nn.Flatten(),
            nn.Linear(28 * 28, 256),
            nn.ReLU(),
            nn.Linear(256, 128),
            nn.ReLU(),
            nn.Linear(128, 10)
        )

    def forward(self, x):
        return self.network(x)</code></pre>

      <p>
        The first operation flattens each image. The first linear layer
        maps 784 input features to 256 hidden features, the second maps
        256 to 128, and the final layer maps 128 features to ten class
        logits.
      </p>

      <h3>10.3 Understand the layer dimensions</h3>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Input features</th><th>Output features</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Flatten</td><td>Image of 28 × 28 pixels</td><td>784</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Linear 1</td><td>784</td><td>256</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Linear 2</td><td>256</td><td>128</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Output linear layer</td><td>128</td><td>10</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        For a batch size of 64, the output shape of the final layer is
        \((64,10)\). Each row contains ten scores for one image.
      </p>

      <h3>10.4 Count the trainable parameters</h3>

      <p>
        A fully connected layer with \(n_{\text{in}}\) input features and
        \(n_{\text{out}}\) output features has:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\text{Parameters}=n_{\text{in}}n_{\text{out}}+n_{\text{out}}\)
      </p>

      <p>
        The first term counts weights, and the second counts biases.
      </p>

      <p>For the example model:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>First layer: \(784(256)+256=200{,}960\)</li>
        <li>Second layer: \(256(128)+128=32{,}896\)</li>
        <li>Output layer: \(128(10)+10=1{,}290\)</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Total trainable parameters:</p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(200{,}960+32{,}896+1{,}290=235{,}146\)
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the parameter count for the example architecture above,
        not a claim that every course implementation uses these exact
        hidden-layer sizes.
      </p>

      <h3>10.5 Why are there ten output units?</h3>

      <p>
        Fashion MNIST has ten classes, so the final layer produces ten
        class scores. A model can select the class with the highest score
        using <code>argmax</code>.
      </p>

      <pre><code>model = FashionMLP()

images, labels = next(iter(train_loader))
logits = model(images)

print(images.shape)  # Typically [64, 1, 28, 28]
print(logits.shape)  # [64, 10]

predicted_classes = logits.argmax(dim=1)
print(predicted_classes.shape)  # [64]</code></pre>

      <p>
        The ten values are logits, not necessarily probabilities. When
        using <code>CrossEntropyLoss</code>, pass these logits directly
        into the loss function.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="fashion-forward">
      <h2>11. Fashion MNIST forward pass</h2>

      <h3>11.1 Forward-pass pipeline</h3>

      <ol>
        <li>Load a batch of images.</li>
        <li>Flatten each image from 28 × 28 pixels into 784 values.</li>
        <li>Apply the first linear transformation.</li>
        <li>Apply ReLU to introduce nonlinearity.</li>
        <li>Apply the next linear transformation and ReLU.</li>
        <li>Calculate ten output logits.</li>
        <li>Compare the logits with the true class labels using a loss function.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>11.2 Understand logits and predictions</h3>

      <p>
        Suppose a model produces the logits:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \([1.2,\ -0.5,\ 0.8,\ 2.1,\ 0.1,\ -1.0,\ 0.4,\ 0.7,\ -0.2,\ 1.0]\)
      </p>

      <p>
        The largest score is 2.1 at index 3. Using zero-based indexing,
        <code>argmax</code> predicts class 3, which corresponds to
        <em>Dress</em> in the standard Fashion MNIST class ordering.
      </p>

      <p>
        The largest logit gives the predicted class even before applying
        softmax, because softmax preserves the ordering of the logits.
        However, the logit itself is not a probability.
      </p>

      <h3>11.3 Cross-entropy with logits</h3>

      <pre><code>criterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss()

logits = model(images)  # Shape: [batch_size, 10]
loss = criterion(logits, labels)</code></pre>

      <p>
        The labels are normally integer class indices with shape
        \((B,)\). Do not apply <code>argmax</code> before calculating
        the loss: doing so discards the information needed for
        differentiable training.
      </p>

      <h3>11.4 Calculate batch accuracy</h3>

      <pre><code>predictions = logits.argmax(dim=1)
correct = (predictions == labels).sum().item()
accuracy = correct / labels.size(0)</code></pre>

      <p>
        If 52 out of 64 images are classified correctly, the batch
        accuracy is:
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\frac{52}{64}\times100=81.25\%\)
      </p>

      <p>
        For a complete dataset, aggregate the number of correct
        predictions and the number of examples across all batches.
        Do not simply average batch accuracies if the batches have
        different sizes.
      </p>

      <h3>Original GA-style example</h3>

      <p><strong>Question:</strong> A Fashion MNIST MLP receives a batch of 20 images. Its final layer has ten output units. What is the expected shape of the logits?</p>

      <ol type="A">
        <li>\((20,1)\)</li>
        <li>\((20,10)\)</li>
        <li>\((10,20)\)</li>
        <li>\((20,784)\)</li>
      </ol>

      <details>
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          <strong>Answer: B.</strong> Each of the 20 images produces
          ten class scores, so the output shape is \((20,10)\).
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section id="fashion-training">
      <h2>12. Fashion MNIST training loop</h2>

      <h3>12.1 The standard training-loop sequence</h3>

      <p>
        The training loop processes one batch at a time. Each batch
        produces predictions and a loss, then backpropagation calculates
        gradients and the optimizer updates the model.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>Set training mode.</li>
        <li>Fetch a batch of images and labels.</li>
        <li>Clear old gradients.</li>
        <li>Run the forward pass.</li>
        <li>Calculate the loss.</li>
        <li>Run the backward pass.</li>
        <li>Update model parameters.</li>
        <li>Accumulate metrics for the epoch.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>12.2 Complete training-loop example</h3>

      <pre><code>device = torch.device(
    "cuda" if torch.cuda.is_available() else "cpu"
)

model = FashionMLP().to(device)
criterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss()
optimizer = torch.optim.Adam(
    model.parameters(),
    lr=0.001
)

num_epochs = 5

for epoch in range(num_epochs):
    model.train()

    running_loss = 0.0
    correct = 0
    total = 0

    for images, labels in train_loader:
        images = images.to(device)
        labels = labels.to(device)

        optimizer.zero_grad()

        logits = model(images)
        loss = criterion(logits, labels)

        loss.backward()
        optimizer.step()

        running_loss += loss.item() * images.size(0)

        predictions = logits.argmax(dim=1)
        correct += (predictions == labels).sum().item()
        total += labels.size(0)

    epoch_loss = running_loss / total
    epoch_accuracy = correct / total

    print(
        f"Epoch {epoch + 1}: "
        f"loss={epoch_loss:.4f}, "
        f"accuracy={epoch_accuracy:.4f}"
    )</code></pre>

      <p>
        This is a general example. It assumes the data loader returns
        tensors that can be moved to the selected device, and the model
        architecture is the one defined earlier.
      </p>

      <h3>12.3 Why multiply the batch loss by batch size?</h3>

      <p>
        Many PyTorch loss functions return a mean loss across the batch
        by default. To calculate an example-weighted average over the
        whole epoch, multiply each mean batch loss by its batch size,
        sum those values, and divide by the total number of examples.
      </p>

      <p class="eq">
        \(\text{Epoch loss}=
        \frac{\sum_b \text{mean loss}_b\times n_b}
        {\sum_b n_b}\)
      </p>

      <p>
        This matters if the last batch is smaller than the others.
      </p>

      <h3>12.4 What is an epoch?</h3>

      <p>
        One epoch is one pass through all training examples. If there
        are 600 examples and the batch size is 100, an epoch normally
        contains six batches.
      </p>

      <p>
        Training for five epochs means that the model has processed the
        training set five times, although the order may change between
        epochs if the loader shuffles the examples.
      </p>

      <h3>12.5 Why do we shuffle the training data?</h3>

      <p>
        Shuffling changes the order in which examples appear during
        training. It helps avoid systematic patterns caused by the
        original ordering of the dataset and changes the composition
        of mini-batches across epochs.
      </p>

      <h3>12.6 Common training-loop mistakes</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>Forgetting <code>optimizer.zero_grad()</code> and unintentionally accumulating gradients.</li>
        <li>Calling <code>optimizer.step()</code> before <code>loss.backward()</code>.</li>
        <li>Using a loss function that does not match the task or target format.</li>
        <li>Moving the model to the GPU but leaving the input tensors on the CPU.</li>
        <li>Using the wrong output or label shapes.</li>
        <li>Reporting only the last batch's loss as the whole epoch's loss.</li>
        <li>Forgetting to return the model to training mode after evaluation.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Memorize this order:</strong>
        <code>zero_grad → forward → loss → backward → step</code>.
      </div>
    </section>

