    <section id="fashion-validation">
      <h2>14. Fashion MNIST validation loop</h2>

      <h3>14.1 Why validate?</h3>

      <p>
        Training loss tells us how well the model fits the training data.
        Validation metrics help estimate how well the model performs on
        held-out examples and can reveal overfitting.
      </p>

      <p>
        A model can continue to improve on training examples while its
        performance on validation examples stops improving. Monitoring
        both helps guide decisions about model size, regularization,
        learning rate, and training duration.
      </p>

      <h3>14.2 Validation-loop example</h3>

      <pre><code>model.eval()

val_loss_total = 0.0
correct = 0
total = 0

with torch.no_grad():
    for images, labels in val_loader:
        images = images.to(device)
        labels = labels.to(device)

        logits = model(images)
        loss = criterion(logits, labels)

        val_loss_total += loss.item() * images.size(0)

        predictions = logits.argmax(dim=1)
        correct += (predictions == labels).sum().item()
        total += labels.size(0)

val_loss = val_loss_total / total
val_accuracy = correct / total

print(f"Validation loss: {val_loss:.4f}")
print(f"Validation accuracy: {val_accuracy:.4f}")</code></pre>

      <p>
        This example assumes <code>val_loader</code>, <code>device</code>,
        <code>criterion</code>, and <code>model</code> have already been
        defined.
      </p>

      <h3>14.3 Why use both <code>eval()</code> and <code>no_grad()</code>?</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><code>model.eval()</code> changes the behaviour of layers such as dropout and batch normalization where applicable.</li>
        <li><code>torch.no_grad()</code> disables gradient tracking in the enclosed operations, reducing memory use and computation.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Calling only one of these does not automatically perform the
        role of the other.
      </p>

      <h3>14.4 Training and validation patterns</h3>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Training loss</th><th>Validation loss</th><th>Possible interpretation</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>High</td><td>High</td><td>The model may be underfitting, not trained enough, or poorly configured.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Decreasing</td><td>Decreasing</td><td>The model may be learning useful patterns that generalize.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Decreasing</td><td>Increasing</td><td>The model may be overfitting.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Low</td><td>Low</td><td>The model fits the training data and performs well on the validation data.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        These are common patterns, not infallible rules. The interpretation
        depends on the task, data distribution, metric, and experimental
        setup.
      </p>

      <h3>14.5 Model selection</h3>

      <p>
        A common approach is to monitor validation loss after each epoch
        and retain the model with the best validation performance. This
        avoids assuming that the final epoch is always the best one.
      </p>

      <pre><code>best_val_loss = float("inf")
best_state = None

# After computing val_loss for an epoch:
if val_loss < best_val_loss:
    best_val_loss = val_loss
    best_state = {
        name: tensor.detach().cpu().clone()
        for name, tensor in model.state_dict().items()
    }

# After training:
if best_state is not None:
    model.load_state_dict(best_state)</code></pre>

      <p>
        This example keeps the best model state in memory. It can be
        saved to disk as well if the workflow requires a persistent
        checkpoint.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="model-training">
      <h2>15. Fashion MNIST model training and evaluation</h2>

      <h3>15.1 What should be monitored?</h3>

      <p>
        A training run may track loss and accuracy for both the training
        and validation sets. Loss provides a measure used for optimization,
        while accuracy measures the fraction of examples classified
        correctly.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two models with the same accuracy can have different losses
        because loss functions also reflect how confidently the model
        makes its predictions.
      </p>

      <h3>15.2 Save a trained model</h3>

      <p>
        A common PyTorch approach is to save the model's state dictionary,
        which contains its learned parameters.
      </p>

      <pre><code>torch.save(model.state_dict(), "fashion_mlp.pth")</code></pre>

      <p>
        To reload the parameters, create the same architecture and load
        the saved state:
      </p>

      <pre><code>model = FashionMLP()
model.load_state_dict(
    torch.load("fashion_mlp.pth", map_location=device)
)
model.to(device)
model.eval()</code></pre>

      <p>
        The architecture must match the saved parameter shapes. A
        state dictionary stores parameter values; it does not by itself
        describe every detail of the model's architecture or training
        history.
      </p>

      <h3>15.3 Reproducibility</h3>

      <p>
        Neural network training may involve randomized weight
        initialization, shuffled data, and other random operations.
        Fixing random seeds can help make experiments more reproducible.
      </p>

      <pre><code>import random
import numpy as np
import torch

seed = 42

random.seed(seed)
np.random.seed(seed)
torch.manual_seed(seed)

if torch.cuda.is_available():
    torch.cuda.manual_seed_all(seed)</code></pre>

      <p>
        A fixed seed helps control randomness but does not guarantee
        bit-for-bit identical results across all devices, library
        versions, or computational operations.
      </p>

      <h3>15.4 Common problems and what they mean</h3>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Problem</th><th>Likely cause or check</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Matrix shapes cannot be multiplied</td><td>Check input feature count, flattening, and linear-layer dimensions.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Expected all tensors on the same device</td><td>Move the model, inputs, and relevant targets to a compatible device.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Loss expects integer class labels</td><td>Check whether the classification loss expects class-index targets.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Binary loss target shape mismatch</td><td>Check the output-logit shape and floating-point target shape.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Loss does not decrease</td><td>Inspect learning rate, data, target encoding, loss choice, gradients, and model configuration.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Training accuracy rises but validation performance falls</td><td>Consider overfitting and review validation metrics.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>15.5 Complete conceptual pipeline</h3>

      <p>
        The entire Fashion MNIST process can be summarized as:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>Load and preprocess images.</li>
        <li>Create data loaders.</li>
        <li>Define the MLP.</li>
        <li>Choose a loss function and optimizer.</li>
        <li>Train over multiple epochs.</li>
        <li>Validate without updating parameters.</li>
        <li>Compare metrics and select a suitable model.</li>
        <li>Save the model if needed.</li>
        <li>Evaluate on a separate test set when the model-selection process is complete.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

