      <h3>Question 1 — Gradient descent</h3>

      <p>
        A weight is initially 5. The gradient is 0.4 and the learning
        rate is 0.25. What is the updated weight?
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          \(w_{\text{new}}=5-(0.25)(0.4)=4.9\).
          <strong>Answer: 4.9.</strong>
        </p>
      </details>

      <h3>Question 2 — Negative gradient</h3>

      <p>
        If the current weight is 1.2, the learning rate is 0.1, and
        the gradient is -0.5, does the weight increase or decrease?
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          \(w_{\text{new}}=1.2-(0.1)(-0.5)=1.25\).
          The weight increases by 0.05.
        </p>
      </details>

      <h3>Question 3 — Universal approximation</h3>

      <p>
        Why are nonlinear activations important when stacking neural
        network layers?
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          Without nonlinear activations, a composition of linear layers
          is equivalent to a single linear transformation. Nonlinear
          activations allow the network to represent nonlinear
          input-output relationships.
        </p>
      </details>

      <h3>Question 4 — MSE calculation</h3>

      <p>
        Targets are \([2,4,6]\), and predictions are \([3,2,7]\).
        Calculate the MSE.
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          The squared errors are \(1,4,1\). Therefore,
          \(\operatorname{MSE}=(1+4+1)/3=2\).
          <strong>Answer: 2.</strong>
        </p>
      </details>

      <h3>Question 5 — Backpropagation</h3>

      <p>
        Given \(u=5x\) and \(y=u^2\), calculate
        \(\frac{dy}{dx}\) at \(x=2\).
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          \(u=10\), so \(\frac{dy}{du}=2u=20\), while
          \(\frac{du}{dx}=5\). Thus,
          \(\frac{dy}{dx}=20(5)=100\).
        </p>
      </details>

      <h3>Question 6 — Churn classification</h3>

      <p>
        A model predicts whether each customer will churn, with one
        binary target per customer. Which loss is a standard choice
        when the model returns one logit per customer?
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          <code>nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss</code>, with floating-point targets
          shaped compatibly with the logits.
        </p>
      </details>

      <h3>Question 7 — Fashion MNIST dimensions</h3>

      <p>
        A linear layer maps 784 features to 100 neurons. How many
        trainable weights and biases does it have?
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          Weights: \(784(100)=78{,}400\). Biases: \(100\).
          Total: <strong>78,500 parameters.</strong>
        </p>
      </details>

      <h3>Question 8 — Cross-entropy</h3>

      <p>
        Why should the output of a ten-class classifier usually be
        passed as logits directly to <code>nn.CrossEntropyLoss</code>?
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          <code>nn.CrossEntropyLoss</code> performs the appropriate
          log-softmax and loss calculation internally. Passing logits
          directly is the standard numerically stable approach.
        </p>
      </details>

      <h3>Question 9 — Validation</h3>

      <p>
        Does <code>model.eval()</code> automatically disable gradient
        tracking?
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          No. <code>model.eval()</code> changes the behaviour of
          certain layers. Use <code>torch.no_grad()</code> or another
          suitable gradient-control context to disable gradient tracking
          during ordinary validation.
        </p>
      </details>

      <h3>Question 10 — Overfitting</h3>

      <p>
        Training accuracy is 99%, but validation accuracy is 72%.
        What should you investigate?
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          The large gap may indicate overfitting or a mismatch between
          training and validation data. Check data quality, split
          consistency, leakage, model complexity, regularization, and
          training duration before deciding on a remedy.
        </p>
      </details>

      <h3>Question 11 — XOR</h3>

      <p>
        What is the XOR output for inputs \((1,1)\), and why is XOR
        important in neural network theory?
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          The output is 0 because the inputs are the same. XOR is an
          important example of a non-linearly separable problem that
          cannot be solved by a single linear decision boundary but
          can be represented by a suitable multilayer network.
        </p>
      </details>

      <h3>Question 12 — Mini-batches</h3>

      <p>
        A training dataset has 1,200 examples and a batch size of 100.
        How many batches are there in one epoch if every example is
        processed exactly once?
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>Answer</summary>
        <p class="answer">
          \(1200/100=12\). There are <strong>12 batches per epoch</strong>.
        </p>
      </details>

