    <section id="overview"><h2>Why vanilla RNNs are extended</h2>
<p>Vanilla RNNs can struggle to preserve information across long sequences because gradients may vanish. LSTM and GRU networks introduce gates that control what information is retained, forgotten, and exposed. Their purpose is not to eliminate every training problem, but to make useful long-range dependencies easier to learn.</p>
</section>
<section id="lstm"><h2>LSTM: cell state and gates</h2>
<p>An LSTM maintains a hidden state \(h_t\) and a cell state \(c_t\). Gates are values between 0 and 1, typically produced by sigmoid functions. A gate near 0 suppresses information; a gate near 1 passes it through.</p><div class="eq">\(f_t=\sigma(W_f[x_t,h_{t-1}]+b_f)\)</div><div class="eq">\(i_t=\sigma(W_i[x_t,h_{t-1}]+b_i),\quad \tilde c_t=\tanh(W_c[x_t,h_{t-1}]+b_c)\)</div><div class="eq">\(c_t=f_t\odot c_{t-1}+i_t\odot\tilde c_t\)</div><div class="eq">\(o_t=\sigma(W_o[x_t,h_{t-1}]+b_o),\quad h_t=o_t\odot\tanh(c_t)\)</div><p><strong>Forget gate</strong> controls old cell information; <strong>input gate</strong> controls candidate information written into the cell; <strong>output gate</strong> controls how much cell state becomes the hidden output. The cell state has an additive update path that can help gradients flow.</p>
</section>
<section id="gru"><h2>GRU: a simpler gated alternative</h2>
<p>A GRU combines memory and hidden representation rather than maintaining separate cell and hidden states. It commonly uses an <strong>update gate</strong> to control how much previous information is retained and a <strong>reset gate</strong> to control how much past state influences the candidate state. GRUs often have fewer parameters than LSTMs because they use fewer gates.</p><p>There is no universal winner: LSTM and GRU performance depends on the dataset, sequence length, architecture and training setup. Compare validation results rather than assuming the more complex model must be better.</p>
<p>A common GRU formulation uses an update gate \(z_t\), reset gate \(r_t\), candidate hidden state \(\tilde h_t\), and previous state \(h_{t-1}\):</p>
<div class="eq">\(z_t=\sigma(W_z[x_t,h_{t-1}]+b_z)\)<br>\(r_t=\sigma(W_r[x_t,h_{t-1}]+b_r)\)<br>\(\tilde h_t=\tanh(W_h[x_t,r_t\odot h_{t-1}]+b_h)\)<br>\(h_t=(1-z_t)\odot h_{t-1}+z_t\odot\tilde h_t\)</div>
<p>Notation varies across implementations: some sources swap the update-gate convention in the final equation. Follow the formula used by your lecture or library when solving a numeric problem. The conceptual roles remain: reset controls how much prior state informs the candidate, and update controls the balance between old and candidate state.</p>
</section>
\n<section id="applications"><h2>Sentiment analysis and time-series prediction</h2>
<p>For sentiment analysis, text is tokenized and mapped to embeddings; an RNN/LSTM/GRU processes the token sequence, and a classifier predicts a label such as positive or negative. For time-series forecasting, past observations are fed in order to predict a future value or sequence.</p><p>Fit preprocessing only on training data where appropriate, split time series chronologically to avoid leakage, and keep sequence windows aligned with their target times. For binary classification, sigmoid plus binary cross-entropy is common; for one-of-K classes, use class logits with categorical cross-entropy or a framework's equivalent logits-based loss.</p>
</section>
<section id="questions"><h2>Week 8 questions and solutions</h2>
<p>Original practice based on the supplied LSTM/GRU slides and sequence-model notebooks.</p><details class="question"><summary>Q1. What does the LSTM forget gate control?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> How much of the previous cell state is retained.</p><p>The forget gate multiplies the previous cell state elementwise; values near zero discard more, while values near one retain more.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q2. Which gate controls how much candidate information is written to the cell state?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> The input gate.</p><p>The input gate scales the candidate cell update before it is added to the retained old cell state.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q3. What are the two main states in a standard LSTM?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Hidden state h_t and cell state c_t.</p><p>The cell state carries longer-term memory, while the hidden state is the exposed representation/output at the current step.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q4. Which gates are commonly used by a GRU?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Update gate and reset gate.</p><p>The update gate controls retention versus replacement, and the reset gate controls the influence of prior state on the candidate.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q5. Why can LSTM/GRU help on long sequences compared with a vanilla RNN?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Gated state updates provide better control over information flow.</p><p>The gated/additive memory paths can help preserve useful information and improve gradient flow.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q6. A binary sentiment model outputs one probability for positive sentiment. Which output/loss pairing is common?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> One sigmoid output with binary cross-entropy.</p><p>The sigmoid produces a probability in (0,1) and binary cross-entropy scores the target label.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q7. Why should a time-series dataset usually be split chronologically?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> To avoid training on future observations relative to validation/test data.</p><p>A random split can leak future distribution information into the training process and overstate forecasting performance.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q8. True or false: an LSTM always outperforms a GRU.</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> False</p><p>Performance depends on data, hyperparameters and training. GRUs can match or outperform LSTMs while using fewer parameters.</p></div></details>
</section>

